# The window needs GTK 4 and libadwaita through GObject introspection, and
# `gearbench-ctl gui` says what is missing and exits 1 without them. The
# daemon runs without python3-gi, its desktop session and login screen check
# off, and the journal says python3-gi is missing when the service starts.
# So these are Recommends: apt installs them by default and a headless box
# can leave them out.
Recommends: python3-gi,
            gir1.2-gtk-4.0,
            gir1.2-adw-1
Description: lighting, G keys, DPI and macros for Logitech G keyboards, mice and headsets
 Gearbench drives Logitech G keyboards and mice over their vendor HID++ 2.0
 interfaces, on a cable or through a LIGHTSPEED receiver, and probes what
 each one can do from its own feature table. The README's table lists the
 models. Those not yet tested on the hardware are these: the G515
 LIGHTSPEED TKL, G915 TKL, G815, PRO X TKL LIGHTSPEED, G213 Prodigy,
 G915 X, G915 X LIGHTSPEED TKL, G316 X, G316 X 98,
 G512 X 75 and G512 X 98 keyboards and the G502 X, G502 X
 LIGHTSPEED, G502 X PLUS MILLENNIUM FALCON, G502 LIGHTSPEED, PRO X
 SUPERLIGHT, PRO WIRELESS, PRO X SUPERLIGHT 2, PRO X SUPERLIGHT 2 DEX, PRO X
 SUPERLIGHT 2c, PRO X2 SUPERSTRIKE, G203 LIGHTSYNC and G903 LIGHTSPEED HERO
 mice, the G431, G433, G533, G535, G633, G635, G733, G935 and PRO X
 Wireless headsets, the Astro A50 Gen 5 headset, and the
 PRO X 2 LIGHTSPEED, G522 LIGHTSPEED and G325 LIGHTSPEED headsets. It
 remaps the G keys and the MR shift layer, sets per-key and zone lighting,
 records and replays macros through a uinput virtual keyboard, binds the
 extra buttons of each mouse but the G305, and sets each mouse's DPI and
 report rate and, on the G502 X PLUS, G305 and G502 HERO, its DPI stages.
 .
 It also drives the Astro A50 Gen 4 base station over its own vendor HID
 interface, which is not HID++: microphone level, sidetone, noise gate and
 mic EQ, the three EQ presets (Astro Command Center's .astroeq files too),
 the stream port mix and the base station's own settings, and it reads the
 headset's battery. It lists the A50 Gen 4's and the A50 X's Game and Chat as
 two outputs.
 .
 For the Logitech G432 it sets the sidetone, and adds an equalizer output
 and noise suppression for its microphone. It does the same for the G432's
 ten relatives, with the equalizer in the headset on the G633, G933 and
 G935, drives the lights of the G633, G635, G733, G933 and G935, and reads
 the wireless ones' battery. Gearbench Spatial, a 7.1 output for games
 rendered for headphones with SADIE II's measured heads, plays to each
 headset Gearbench drives and is off until switched on.
 .
 It sets the Astro A50 X through its base station: the sidetone, volume,
 Game and Voice balance, noise gate, auto sleep, tones, the base station's
 brightness and deep sleep, the stream mix and the headset's equalizers,
 and reads its battery. It reads the same of the Astro A50 Gen 5, less the
 deep sleep. It reads the PRO X 2 LIGHTSPEED through its receiver: the
 battery, sidetone, auto sleep, the microphone's noise reduction and the
 headset's equalizer. It reads the G522 LIGHTSPEED
 through its receiver or on its cable, and the G325 LIGHTSPEED through its
 receiver: the battery, sidetone, auto sleep, eco mode and the headset's
 equalizer, and the G325's microphone noise reduction. It changes nothing
 on any of them but the A50 X until each change is tested.
 .
 A user systemd service owns the devices and holds the keyboard's software
 lighting claim; the command line and the GTK 4 window are front ends that talk
 to it over a unix socket. Nothing runs as root. Device access comes from
 udev rule files that tag nodes with uaccess, which grants them to the user
 of the active seat: the control interface of each keyboard, on its cable
 or its receiver, of each cabled mouse, of the A50's base station, of the
 G432's adapter, of the A50 X's and A50 Gen 5's base station, of the PRO
 X 2 LIGHTSPEED's, G522 LIGHTSPEED's and G325 LIGHTSPEED's receivers and of
 the G522 on its cable, and the
 node a receiver gives a device paired to it. The HID interface of the
 G431's and G433's adapters, of the G633's and G635's cables, of the G533's
 and G535's receivers, of the G733's and G933's receivers and of the G935's
 and PRO X Wireless's receivers is granted too: on the G432 it carries the
 volume wheel and the mute beside HID++, and no keystrokes.
 A mouse's node behind its receiver carries its motion and buttons too
 and, going by the kernel's source, the keys its buttons are bound to type,
 and would carry the keystrokes of a keyboard paired to the same receiver
 after the mouse, so pair nothing else there. The G815's, G813's
 and G213 Prodigy's control interfaces and the nodes of the G515
 LIGHTSPEED TKL, G915 TKL, G913 TKL, PRO X TKL LIGHTSPEED, G915 X LIGHTSPEED and
 G915 X LIGHTSPEED TKL behind their receivers
 carry what is typed on them, and the G502 HERO's,
 G502 X's, G203 LIGHTSYNC's and G102 LIGHTSYNC's control interfaces the keys
 their buttons are bound to type,
 so with those rules any program that user runs can read them. A keyboard
 paired to one of those receivers after the keyboard on it would, going by
 the kernel's source, have its keystrokes arrive on that keyboard's node
 too, so pair no other keyboard there. The control
 interfaces of the G515 TKL, the G515 LIGHTSPEED TKL on its cable and the
 G915 X, G316 X and G512 X models carry each press and release of Ctrl,
 Shift, Alt, Super and FN too, from the time Gearbench sets the keyboard up
 until it loses power: switched off, or unplugged where it has no battery.
 Any program that user runs can turn that on itself and read them. A program
 that opened such a node keeps reading it after another account takes the
 seat, and so reads what that account types. Another file tags /dev/uinput
 too, the line steam-devices ships. With it any program that user runs can
 create a virtual keyboard and type into the session, and one that opened
 it can keep that keyboard after another account takes the seat and type
 into that account's session, a sudo prompt included. On a machine several
 people use, the group grant from the comment of
 /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/70-gearbench-uinput.rules, put into a copy of each
 rule in /etc/udev/rules.d/, limits the rules' grants to the group's
 accounts. The G502 X PLUS's own event nodes are the gearbench group's to
 read, which the package creates; with host mode off, the extra buttons
 need the user's account in it. A program of any account in the group that
 has one open can still take the mouse from the seat user's session until
 it lets go, or change what its buttons send until the mouse is plugged in
 again.
 .
 The keyboard's onboard profile is never written, but the G515 TKL and the
 G915 X LIGHTSPEED keep the brightness they are sent through a power-off,
 and the G515 LIGHTSPEED TKL is taken to. Five mouse commands do write a
 mouse's onboard profile 1, each backing up every profile sector first and
 reading the sector back after. The A50 Gen 4's settings reach its base
 station's flash only when saved. The A50 X keeps each setting written to it
 through a power cycle, the G432's adapter keeps its sidetone through an
 unplug, and the G432's relatives may keep theirs.
